00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
what do you think  is going through  mr putin's mind this morning  well that's obviously the key question  that we're all trying to work out and i  think what we have to appreciate is the  extent to which putin is not the kind of  person who  fixes himself in a particular  track and timetable and rigorously  sticks to that he clearly believes in  creating options for himself and then  constantly reassessing them so i mean  what he will be doing today is we're  told that there is this war plan  that would actually be triggered on  wednesday  maybe maybe not but on the other hand  you know he'll be looking at well what  are the likely costs of actions what are  the likely benefits so this is it i mean  i think he will constantly be  reassessing that and i think that is why  it's so important that we keep up both  the the pressure  but also make it clear that there are  off ramps because unfortunately if we  give him an option which is essentially  between escalation and capitulation then  he will probably escalate  what um what does an off-ramp look like  in this context that's a very  interesting concept  well what we have to realize is that  first of all russia believes that it has  some genuine security concerns now we  may disagree with a lot of the basic  precepts about whether nato is  aggressive and so forth but just simply  telling the russians that they're wrong  is not going to get them to say oh my  apologies we'll back away so they have  to have  an opportunity to basically talk through  their various issues  secondly there are a lot of specific  issues many which frankly the americans  have already brought on to the table  that in some ways what we have to do is  talk them up as concessions to the  russians it may well and it does stick  in the craw to in effect act as  president putin's pr agents but in some  ways we we must get away from a  situation in which we're trying to push  him into a position where he will feel  that he's been defeated and instead we  want him to feel that what we're  offering which does not in any way  infringe on ukrainian sovereignty and  the wider european security architecture  but what is already there actually is a  genuine step forward for him so a lot of  this is frankly smoke and mirrors which  is after all what diplomacy is often all  about  well it's very interesting insight into  mr putin's psychology i talked to  mikhail kordekovsky the  russian billionaire and former friend i  suppose uh or associated of mr putin and  he  said that he was a little bit paranoid  and  um  putin once said i haven't got quote in  front of me but i think putin once said  that the um the russian bear could be  quiet but on the other hand  uh if it's  stayed quiet then its enemies would be  uh tearing its claws and fangs out and  that therefore  i suppose in a way they had to be  aggressive  what do you think about that assessment  putin basically  uh is not just smart but also paranoid  i think there is a lot of paranoia in  not just putin but a lot of people  around him remember these people in  their late 60s and early 70s a lot of  them veterans of the kgb all of them  though products of the old circuit  system so they still have a lot of the  old attitudes and they're also the ones  who are probably most caught by the  traumatic end of empire which is  metastasized from uh what have we lost  to uh and who took it from us so yes  there there is a very toxic  perspective on the west there but go  back to the point about the  the russian bear is look  unfortunately the russians have  internalized rightfully a sense that the  west only pays attention to russia when  russia causes problems for the west  and there's a certain amount of truth in  that and unfortunately that means  obviously without any way excusing what  putin is doing we shouldn't be surprised  that the russians continue to make  problems for us because that's how they  they get our attention but i think that  is the key thing if all he wanted to do  was crack the whip on ukraine he could  have done it by now he could have done  it much more quickly and with with much  less of this kind of lengthy almost  theatrical buildup the fact that we have  this is a sign that at least putin is  hoping that he can get something through  negotiation rather than having to  unleash the troops with all the massive  political economic and social costs that  would involve  so that really leads us to the key  question  how likely do you think an invasion is  it sounds as though you think  not very likely  i mean that that is the question and  unfortunately then no doubt i will be  proven wrong whatever i i predict and i  think this is the thing at the moment  it's only putin who gets to decide and  we don't know what he's thinking for  certain my view is that invasion or some  substantial military escalation is not  his plan a  he's absolutely hoping to use all these  other methods intimidation diplomacy  covert attacks and so forth to get  enough of concessions that he feels he's  actually managed to make his point and  put russia at the center of the world  again which is where he thinks it should  be  that doesn't mean though that military  escalation is not there as a plan b or a  plan c as an option if he really feels  that he's not going to get anything  anywhere then anyway he will probably  feel he has little option but to  escalate to a degree maybe not these  kind of apocalyptic scenarios we've been  hearing about rolling the tanks around  kiev and so forth but some kind of  military escalation just to make a point  but as i said i think that that's still  plan b  if i was going to guess i think there's  going to be a war on wednesday  i will say not  sorry um  last question i guess um  in this rather delicate  balance of decision making  how  helpful is the  defense secretary's reference to munich  and essentially appeasement  i mean it's unfortunate not least  because it obviously cursed the russians  as nazi germany which you know for a  country which lost so much in the second  world war which still calls it the great  patriotic war that's a very neuralgic  point but i mean i think in in fairness  the russians are sophisticated enough to  realize that there is the the dialogue  that you have with your own domestic  audience and the dialogue you have with  other countries and in fact ben  wallace's visit to moscow seems to have  actually been pretty successful it was  very sober it wasn't a  highly publicized spat it was actually  two people trying to talk about  challenges and also the the parallel and  very important talks between chief of  the defense staff admiral radekin and  his russian counterpart general  gerasimov so i mean actually in no sense  i think ben wallace has probably  accumulated enough credit that he can be  allowed a few rhetorical flourishes as  far as moscow is concerned

